1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,860
July 1st, 2025, Rio Hurtado,

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:09,000
Chile, just before dawn, a survey telescope built to catch asteroids on a collision course

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,680
with earth flags, something else entirely,

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:14,180
a point of light moving far too fast to belong to our solar system.

5
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:17,260
Astronomers confirm it within days,

6
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:22,880
only the third interstellar object ever detected after one in 2017 and one in

7
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:26,760
2019. Its orbit is the most extreme of the three,

8
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:31,480
tilted almost sideways, moving in the opposite direction of everything else in the solar

9
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,900
system. In the first days after discovery,

10
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:38,800
early brightness estimates suggest that it might be up to 20 kilometers wide,

11
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,400
the largest interstellar object ever seen.

12
00:00:42,220 --> 00:00:45,540
Weeks later, once the Hubble Space Telescope resolved it directly,

13
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:48,260
that number collapsed to somewhere under 6 kilometers.

14
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,920
A Harvard astrophysicist takes one look at the numbers and says,

15
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:55,720
in public, that it might not be a comet at all.

16
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,660
It might be a probe. Four months later,

17
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,380
NASA holds a press conference to answer him directly.

18
00:01:04,100 --> 00:01:07,860
This object is a comet, says NASA Associate Administrator Amit Shatriya.

19
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:10,260
It looks and behaves like a comet,

20
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:12,960
and all evidence points towards it being a comet.

21
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,660
That argument, one scientist convinced we might not be alone,

22
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,000
an entire agency convinced we are,

23
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:23,120
is not new. It is 75 years old,

24
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:26,320
and it started almost by accident at a lunch table.

25
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:28,960
You're watching Mindwide.

26
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:32,660
This is the real story of the Fermi Paradox,

27
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:36,140
the offhand question that became a 75 -year argument,

28
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,600
the real scientists who tried to answer it,

29
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:41,300
and the two live, unresolved cases,

30
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:45,080
one from 2025, one still running in 2026,

31
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:49,780
that show exactly how close and how far we actually are.

32
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,840
Chapter one, the lunch nobody wrote down.

33
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:56,720
Summer, 1950. Los Alamos,

34
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:59,820
New Mexico. At a lunch table inside Fuller Lodge,

35
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:03,000
physicist Enrico Fermi sits down with three colleagues,

36
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:05,340
Emil Konopinski, Edward Teller,

37
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,600
and Herbert York. On the walkover,

38
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:12,680
the four men had been talking about a New Yorker cartoon published a few months earlier,

39
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:17,240
a flying saucer and little green men carrying off the neighborhood's trash cans,

40
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:22,480
poking fun at a real, minor 1950 news story about missing garbage cans in

41
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,000
New York City. Then, with no warning,

42
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:29,620
Fermi asked a question that had nothing to do with anything they'd been discussing.

43
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:31,900
Konopinski remembered it as,

44
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:35,480
but where is everybody? Teller remembered the same four words.

45
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:37,420
York remembered it slightly differently.

46
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:39,440
Don't you ever wonder where everybody is?

47
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,780
Everyone at the table understood immediately.

48
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,300
He meant extraterrestrials.

49
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:47,080
Fermi followed up with his own back -of -the -envelope math,

50
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:52,720
the odds of other things included

51
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:57,680
that, statistically, we should have been visited long ago and many times over.

52
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:01,100
Here's the honest part almost nobody mentions.

53
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,320
The three men who were actually there never agreed on when it happened.

54
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,440
York said 1951. Teller, separately,

55
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,520
said probably before 1950.

56
00:03:11,180 --> 00:03:15,880
A Los Alamos historian spent decades trying to pin the date down and settled on 1950,

57
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:21,000
based on how fresh a cartoon still felt in one man's memory 34 years later.

58
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:25,640
Fuller Lodge is still standing today in downtown Los Alamos.

59
00:03:26,260 --> 00:03:29,460
The actual room where this happened is a museum you can walk into.

60
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:33,580
Chapter two, the name took 27 years to attach.

61
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:36,660
Fermi never published his question.

62
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:40,040
He never gave it a name. The idea just sat there,

63
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:45,200
unrecorded, until a young astronomer named Carl Sagan mentioned it in a footnote 13 years

64
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:50,400
later. In 1975, astronomer Michael Hart published the first

65
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,300
rigorous version of the argument in a British astronomy journal.

66
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,660
His paper never once mentions Fermi's name.

67
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:57,620
It never uses the word paradox.

68
00:03:59,380 --> 00:04:01,720
Hart's argument didn't stop in 1975.

69
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:07,780
In 1980, physicist Frank Tipler extended it into what's now called the Hart -Tipler conjecture,

70
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,800
an even harder -line version of the same case.

71
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:13,920
That same year as Hart's paper,

72
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:18,560
engineer David Viewing published a separate paper that finally called it a paradox and

73
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:21,320
credited Fermi by name for asking the original question.

74
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:27,500
The exact phrase Fermi's paradox didn't appear in print until 1977,

75
00:04:27,940 --> 00:04:30,120
coined by physicist David Stevenson,

76
00:04:30,380 --> 00:04:33,640
who later admitted he only put quotation marks around it as,

77
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:36,680
in his words, an apology for using a short,

78
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:39,580
convenient label for a much messier argument.

79
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:43,880
The man everyone credits never wrote the paradox down.

80
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:46,620
Three other men, spread across two years,

81
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:49,860
built the argument and gave it his name without him.

82
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:54,800
Chapter three, the same argument got a formula 11 years later.

83
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:00,660
A year before that, Frank Drake had already run the very first real search for alien

84
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:02,600
radio signals, Project OSMA,

85
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:04,340
pointed at two nearby stars,

86
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:07,160
spring of 1960. It found nothing,

87
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:10,160
but it proved the search itself was possible.

88
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:14,820
November 1, 1961, Green Bank,

89
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:19,600
West Virginia. Drake gathers ten scientists for a meeting that would define how the search

90
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:21,940
for alien life gets talked about to this day.

91
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:26,880
On the first morning, Drake writes seven variables on a chalkboard.

92
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:29,620
The rate stars form, the fraction with planets,

93
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:31,420
the fraction that could support life,

94
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:34,660
and so on, multiplied together into a single number.

95
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:36,900
It becomes known as the Drake Equation.

96
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:40,340
One variable in that equation is called L.

97
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:43,620
How long a civilization survives before it goes silent?

98
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:45,860
Drake built it that way on purpose.

99
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:50,600
It was 1961. The Berlin Wall had gone up two months earlier.

100
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:55,440
Every scientist at that table was living through the real possibility of nuclear war.

101
00:05:56,540 --> 00:06:01,120
A radio astronomer named Sebastian von Horner had already put a number on that fear.

102
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:06,640
60%, he estimated, was the chance any civilization wipes itself out with nuclear

103
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:08,680
war once it reaches that stage.

104
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:13,700
Soviet astronomer Iosef Shklovski later grew so convinced of it that he renounced the

105
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:15,300
search for alien signals entirely.

106
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:22,280
In 1966, Shklovski and Carl Sagan co -wrote an entire book built around that same

107
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:27,160
uncertain lifespan. Sagan brought the same warning to a mass television audience in 1980

108
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:32,220
on Cosmos. Chapter 4. We finally have real numbers to

109
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:35,360
plug into that formula. For years,

110
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,000
the working number was tiny.

111
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:40,800
Of NASA's Kepler telescope's first thousand confirmed planets,

112
00:06:41,020 --> 00:06:45,040
only about a dozen were even close to Earth's size and sitting in the right zone.

113
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:48,840
For the first four decades after that chalkboard,

114
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:51,980
nobody could fill in Drake's equation with anything but guesses.

115
00:06:52,780 --> 00:06:55,800
There were zero confirmed planets outside our solar system.

116
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:59,040
Then, in 1995, that changed.

117
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:05,800
Thirty years later, NASA has confirmed more than 6 ,000 exoplanets with billions

118
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,720
more believed to exist across the galaxy.

119
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:12,180
Narrow that down to planets actually sitting in the habitable zone,

120
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:14,360
rocky, roughly Earth -sized,

121
00:07:14,780 --> 00:07:16,240
the right distance from their star,

122
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:21,880
and a 2026 catalog from Cornell's Carl Sagan Institute finds exactly 45

123
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:26,020
real candidates. Even that number is a moving target.

124
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:31,060
Depending on the study, the fraction of sun -like stars with a truly Earth -like planet

125
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:34,900
range is anywhere from 1 % to as high as 40%.

126
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:36,800
We've never actually finished counting.

127
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:42,240
We've extrapolated. NASA's newer test telescope isn't overturning that

128
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:43,920
picture. It's sharpening it,

129
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:47,120
tightening the uncertainty around numbers Kepler could only estimate.

130
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:50,520
This is a number actively being worked on right now,

131
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:53,220
not one anybody's finished with.

132
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:57,640
Chapter 5. Is the worst part behind us or still ahead?

133
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:02,360
In 1996, an economist, not an astronomer,

134
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:06,740
named Robin Hanson wrote an essay that reframed the entire question.

135
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:08,480
He called it the Great Filter.

136
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:12,740
Somewhere on the path from dead matter to a galaxy -spanning civilization,

137
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:17,620
at least one step is so improbable that almost nothing ever makes it through.

138
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:22,720
His own words, the easier it was for life to evolve to our stage,

139
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:25,540
the bleaker our future chances probably are.

140
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:27,980
If most of the filter is already behind us,

141
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:31,200
our future is wide open. If it's still ahead,

142
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:33,660
something is coming that we haven't survived yet,

143
00:08:33,860 --> 00:08:37,660
in any case we know of. Philosopher Nick Bostrom,

144
00:08:37,940 --> 00:08:40,080
then at Oxford's Future of Humanity Institute,

145
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:45,200
took the ahead -of -us version seriously enough to publish a 2008 essay literally titled

146
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:48,800
Why I Hope the Search for Extraterrestrial Life Finds Nothing,

147
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,720
among his candidates for a filter still ahead of humanity,

148
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,080
super -intelligent artificial intelligence with destructive goals.

149
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:59,140
Not every scientist agrees a filter is even needed.

150
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:05,040
In 2018, three Oxford researchers argued the paradox might simply dissolve once

151
00:09:05,040 --> 00:09:08,160
you model real statistical uncertainty into Drake's equation,

152
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:10,060
raising the odds that we are simply,

153
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:15,260
quietly, alone in this galaxy to as high as 99 .6%.

154
00:09:15,820 --> 00:09:17,860
Toby Ord, also of Oxford,

155
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:22,880
later estimated our own generation faces roughly a one in six chance of civilisation

156
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:24,580
-ending catastrophe this century,

157
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:29,960
nuclear war, engineered pandemics and unaligned artificial intelligence among the largest

158
00:09:29,960 --> 00:09:33,440
contributors. If a filter really is ahead of every civilisation,

159
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:36,180
we may be standing inside it right now.

160
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:39,840
Chapter six, maybe the rare part isn't life,

161
00:09:40,160 --> 00:09:44,480
it's us. Ward and Brownlee's collaborators went even further,

162
00:09:44,940 --> 00:09:46,720
proposing a galactic habitable zone,

163
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:49,660
the idea that only certain regions of the Milky Way,

164
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:51,780
at the right distance from the crowded,

165
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:53,940
radiation -heavy galactic centre,

166
00:09:54,140 --> 00:09:56,100
can support complex life at all.

167
00:09:56,520 --> 00:10:00,980
One of that paper's own co -authors later became links to intelligent design advocacy

168
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:03,820
in a separate, far more controversial book,

169
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:06,780
a reminder to judge each paper on its own record,

170
00:10:06,980 --> 00:10:10,700
not by association. In the year 2000,

171
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:15,760
geologist Peter Ward and astronomer Donald Brownlee proposed a different answer entirely.

172
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:18,580
Simple microbial life, they argued,

173
00:10:18,940 --> 00:10:21,100
might be everywhere. Complex life,

174
00:10:21,580 --> 00:10:23,040
anything bigger than a microbe,

175
00:10:23,140 --> 00:10:24,760
might be almost impossibly rare.

176
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:28,320
Their case rests on a list of coincidences.

177
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:31,640
A moon just large enough to stabilise our planet's tilt,

178
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:36,700
plate tectonics that recycle carbon and keep the climate survivable for billions of years,

179
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:40,200
a magnetic field shielding the atmosphere from the sun's radiation.

180
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,820
They also argued Jupiter acts as a gravitational shield,

181
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:47,100
sweeping up comets before they reach us.

182
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:50,760
A later three -part study directly challenged that claim.

183
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:54,720
Finding a Jupiter -sized planet doesn't clearly reduce the danger at all.

184
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:56,060
For some kinds of impacts,

185
00:10:56,260 --> 00:10:57,440
it can even make things worse.

186
00:10:58,680 --> 00:11:01,700
Other scientists pushed back hard on the whole idea.

187
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:04,500
One reviewer called it restrictive and unimaginative,

188
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:09,240
a kind of circular reasoning that just describes the one planet we happen to live on

189
00:11:09,240 --> 00:11:12,280
and calls it a rule. Chapter 7.

190
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:16,060
The theory that says silence might be the only rational choice.

191
00:11:17,500 --> 00:11:20,400
In 2008, Chinese novelist Liu Sixin,

192
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:22,340
not a scientist, a novelist,

193
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:27,440
published a science fiction idea that would eventually leak into real scientific debate.

194
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:29,280
He called it the dark forest.

195
00:11:30,200 --> 00:11:32,000
Every civilisation is a silent,

196
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:36,460
armed hunter, unable to ever verify another civilisation's intentions.

197
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,480
So the only rational move is total silence,

198
00:11:39,820 --> 00:11:43,200
and the only rational response to being noticed is to strike first.

199
00:11:44,620 --> 00:11:46,580
The idea itself is older than the novel.

200
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,640
Astronomer David Brin raised a nearly identical,

201
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:52,380
deadly probes concern back in 1983,

202
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:57,400
and it's the exact logic Avi Loeb reached for in 2025 when

203
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:01,820
he floated the possibility that the object from our cold open could be reconnaissance,

204
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:06,720
not just rock and ice. But even Loeb doesn't claim certainty.

205
00:12:07,240 --> 00:12:11,400
In his own words, this object is most likely a comet of natural origin.

206
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:17,200
He rates his own alien technology hypothesis a 4 out of 10 on a scale he invented himself.

207
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:20,240
Penn State astronomer Jason Wright,

208
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:23,340
who runs his own search for alien technology for a living,

209
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,820
published a point -by -point rebuttal of every claimed anomaly.

210
00:12:27,540 --> 00:12:31,260
Two separate radio observatories listened to the object during its closest approach.

211
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:32,760
Neither one heard anything.

212
00:12:34,060 --> 00:12:37,580
A gentler version of the same silence idea came decades earlier.

213
00:12:38,500 --> 00:12:43,280
In 1973, Harvard radio astronomer John Ball proposed the zoo hypothesis.

214
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:48,960
Maybe advanced civilisations already know we're here and are simply choosing not to interfere

215
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:52,840
the way a visitor at a nature reserve watches without stepping inside the fence.

216
00:12:54,480 --> 00:12:57,840
It's a real, sightable, seriously discussed idea.

217
00:12:58,260 --> 00:12:59,900
It's also, by its own logic,

218
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:05,820
impossible to disprove. Any silence at all is consistent with a civilisation that's simply

219
00:13:05,820 --> 00:13:07,180
choosing to watch quietly.

220
00:13:08,860 --> 00:13:13,180
Physicist Stephen Hawking made a similar argument in a very different context back in

221
00:13:13,180 --> 00:13:18,400
2010, warning that broadcasting our location to the universe could be like Native Americans'

222
00:13:18,680 --> 00:13:19,880
first encounter in Columbus.

223
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:21,560
He never used the phrase,

224
00:13:21,940 --> 00:13:26,320
dark forest. The novel that coined it wouldn't be translated into English for another

225
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:30,580
five years. That same caution shows up in a real,

226
00:13:30,780 --> 00:13:32,420
non -fictional policy fight.

227
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:37,820
In the 2010s, several senior scientists linked to the search for alien signals resigned

228
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:42,320
from committees to protest loosened rules on deliberately transmitting messages toward

229
00:13:42,320 --> 00:13:47,060
other stars, arguing that broadcasting our location without asking the rest of the planet

230
00:13:47,060 --> 00:13:50,440
first is a risk no single group has the right to take.

231
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:54,380
Chapter eight, the signal that almost was.

232
00:13:56,240 --> 00:14:00,380
124 light years away, there's a planet called K218b,

233
00:14:00,680 --> 00:14:02,840
bigger than Earth, smaller than Neptune,

234
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:04,980
sitting in its star's habitable zone.

235
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:06,600
For three straight years,

236
00:14:06,860 --> 00:14:10,920
it has been at the centre of the closest real scientific fight over whether we've actually

237
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:12,400
detected life anywhere else.

238
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:19,000
September 2023, Cambridge astronomer Niko Matusudan's team uses the James Webb Space

239
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,340
Telescope and detects methane and carbon dioxide in its atmosphere,

240
00:14:22,580 --> 00:14:25,260
plus a faint, tentative hint of something else,

241
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:27,600
dimethyl sulphide, a gas that,

242
00:14:27,860 --> 00:14:31,180
on Earth, is produced almost exclusively by marine microbes.

243
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:37,760
April 2025, the same team publishes a new observation with a different instrument,

244
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:39,220
reporting the signal again,

